  "raw_label": "appellant_lost",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Britannia Industries Ltd vs Unknown on 18 September, 2024 Author: Pankaj Purohit Bench: Pankaj Purohit Office Notes, reports, Sl. orders or proceedings or Date Court'S Or Judge'S Or No. directions and Registrar's order with Signatures Ao No.376 of 2024",
    "facts": "4. It is case of the appellant that the petition was proceeded by the Adjudicating Officer in Adjudication Case No.51/66 of 2022, State of Uttarakhand Vs. Prabodh Kher under the provisions of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006. The said appeal was filed by the appellant after the prescribed period of limitation i.e., 30 days as prescribed under Rule 3.3.1 of the Food Safety and Standard Rules, 2011 (hereinafter referred to as \"Food Safety Rules, 2011\"). 7. Under Rule 3.1 of the Food Safety Rules, 2011, the Appellate Tribunal has power to condone the delay of maximum 30 days. 8. Since the appeal of the appellant was filed beyond 60 days, therefore the appeal was dismissed as time barred.",
    "arguments": "10. It is submitted by learned counsel for the appellant that the Appellate Tribunal has wrongly dismissed the application under Section 5 of the Limitation Act, 1963, which provides that the provision of Limitation Act, 1963 shall apply to an appeal made to a Tribunal except as otherwise provided in Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006. It is further submitted by learned counsel for the appellant that the Rules cannot override the said provisions of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 and therefore the judgment impugned is bad in law and contrary to the principle of law and if an interpretation is taken in the matter that the Rules can override the express provision of the law then the provision of Section 76(1) of the Food Safety Act, 2006 would be otiose."
  },
